The README shows the Go example uses "+5511987654321" for phone — E.164 with "+". Confirmed by multiple sources: phone keys in the payload should be in E.164 format with country code.

So the fix is clear: for PHONE keys, normalize to E.164 (+55 prefix). Let me update `normalizePixKey`:

```ts
export function normalizePixKey(key: string, keyType?: string): string {
  const t = (keyType || '').toUpperCase();
  const trimmed = key.trim();
  if (t === 'EMAIL') return trimmed.toLowerCase();
  if (t === 'RANDOM') return trimmed;
  let digits = trimmed.replace(/\D/g, '');
  if (t === 'PHONE') {
    // E.164: garante DDI 55 no início (chaves telefone no DICT incluem o código do país)
    if (!digits.startsWith('55')) digits = '55' + digits;
    return '+' + digits;
  }
  return digits; // CPF/CNPJ
}
```

Wait — should I include the "+" in the payload? Sources conflict a bit:
- Bankly: "+5523415162342"
- DICT API XML: "+5561999999999"
- klimadev Go example: "+5511987654321"
- criarqr: "E.164 (`+` seguido de 10 a 15 dígitos)"

All three show "+". I'll include "+". The DICT stores the key with the "+" (CheckKeysResponse shows hasEntry=true for "+5561999999999" and hasEntry=false for "99999999999").

Hmm, but let me double check — is that really how DICT stores? The CheckKeys example: `<Key hasEntry = "false" > 99999999999 </ Key >` — a 11-digit number without + has NO entry; `+5561999999999` HAS entry. That's conclusive: phone keys in DICT include "+55". 

So my fix: for PHONE, output "+5585988014049" — matching what DICT has. This will resolve the "código inválido" error for phone keys.

Also I should add validation on save in the backend (auth profile) to catch format issues early, plus update the hint text in the Configurações UI: "Informe com DDD; o sistema adiciona o +55 automaticamente. Ex: 85988014049".

Also, the user's stored key "85988014049" → normalized to "+5585988014049". Good.

Additionally, there might be the issue that the user's test key isn't registered at all in DICT — I can't check that. But the E.164 fix addresses the format mismatch. I'll also add CPF/CNPJ digit validation? That may be overkill. Let me keep it focused: phone E.164 fix + UI hint.

Let me also check where normalizePixKey is used — in pix.ts generate and in the frontend? Let me grep. Also the frontend configuracoes hint text. And financeiro-list uses generate endpoint (backend). The frontend qrcode display just renders the code from backend. So single fix point in backend pix.ts.

Wait — also need to check: does the backend use normalizePixKey when saving the profile (auth.ts)? Let me check how pixKey is stored — raw user input "85988014049" stored as-is. That's fine; normalization happens at generation time.

One more consideration: generate-pix uses `user.pixKey` + `user.pixKeyType` — and normalizePixKey(key, keyType). PHONE → "+5585988014049". 

Now also — the merchant name in the payload is "DRA. SARAH MILLER" — has a period. EMV spec allows alphanumeric and some punctuation; periods are fine in merchant name? The spec says merchant name is up to 25 chars, "no special characters"... Actually punctuation like "." is commonly accepted (many real payloads contain ".", "-", "&"). Bank apps display it; CRC covers integrity. It shouldn't cause invalidity. But to be safe I could strip periods too... The BCB manual says field 59 should only contain printable ASCII chars in the range 0x20-0x7E... Actually let me check the manual rule: "Campo 59 — Nome do recebedor: texto ASCII (caracteres imprimíveis)". Most generators strip accents only. Periods are fine (real banks' own payloads include e.g. "DRA. MARIA"). I'll leave it.

Let me apply the fix.

